  "scriptId": "td-07-delayed-starts-catch-up-overtaking",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Delayed Starts, Catch-Up, and Overtaking",
    "description": "Turn a head start into a distance gap, close it with relative speed, and solve chases, overtaking times, and catch points.",
    "teachingApproach": "Make every pursuit begin with the head-start distance. Then subtract speeds to get the closing rate. Extend the same picture to trains, thieves and police, clock-time questions, catch locations, shuttle-dog motion, regularly spaced buses, and long delayed starts. Keep the learner from tracking two full journeys when gap ÷ closing speed is enough.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 18, Time and Distance. Approved scope: Cover delayed starts, head-start gaps, catch-up and overtaking, goods/express train patterns, thief-police chases, catch location, clock-time catches, shuttle motion during a pursuit, regularly spaced vehicles, and same-route delayed-departure questions."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn pursuit problems as a head-start story.\n\nA slower mover starts first and creates a gap. The faster mover then closes that gap at the difference of their speeds.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A runner moves at 8 km/hr. A chaser starts 30 minutes later at 12 km/hr.\n\nIn 30 minutes, the runner gets 4 km ahead.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Closing speed = 12 − 8 = 4 km/hr.\n\nCatch time after the chaser starts = 4 ÷ 4 = 1 hour.\n\nEvery pursuit has the same two pieces: head-start gap, then closing speed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "A cyclist moves at 10 km/hr. Another starts 36 minutes later at 16 km/hr. How long after the second cyclist starts will the catch occur?",
